{
  "id": "5-1",
  "title": "Understand the Distributive Property",
  "goal": "I can decompose a fact into two easier facts and add.",
  "catchAll": "Find the first part and the second part. Then add them.",
  "teacher": [
    {
      "term": "Distributive Property",
      "def": "a multiplication fact can be broken apart into the sum of two other related facts"
    }
  ],
  "glossary": {
    "decompose": "break apart into smaller parts",
    "sum": "the answer when you add",
    "area": "how many squares cover a shape"
  },
  "learn": {
    "screens": [
      {
        "title": "Ready? Warm-up",
        "item": {
          "id": "r1",
          "type": "number",
          "prompt": "How many dots?",
          "model": {
            "type": "array",
            "rows": 5,
            "cols": 3
          },
          "answer": "5 × 3",
          "hints": [
            "There are 5 rows. Each row has 3 dots.",
            "Count by 3s: 3, 6, 9, 12, …"
          ],
          "mistakes": [
            {
              "when": {
                "answer": 8
              },
              "say": "5 + 3 is 8. Count the dots: 5 rows of 3."
            }
          ],
          "yes": "Yes! 5 rows of 3 is 15."
        }
      },
      {
        "title": "Ready? One more",
        "item": {
          "id": "r2",
          "type": "number",
          "prompt": "1 × 3 = ?",
          "answer": "1 × 3",
          "hints": [
            "1 × 3 means 1 group of 3."
          ],
          "mistakes": [
            {
              "when": {
                "answer": 4
              },
              "say": "1 + 3 is 4, but this is times. 1 group of 3 is 3."
            }
          ],
          "yes": "Yes! 1 group of 3 is 3."
        }
      },
      {
        "title": "Decompose a fact",
        "text": [
          "6 × 3 means 6 groups of 3. We draw it as 6 rows of 3 dots.",
          "That is a lot to count. So we decompose it: we break it into two smaller facts we already know.",
          "Cut under row 5. The top part is 5 × 3. The bottom part is 1 × 3."
        ],
        "model": {
          "type": "array",
          "rows": 6,
          "cols": 3,
          "cut": {
            "by": "rows",
            "at": 5
          },
          "labels": "facts"
        },
        "caption": "6 × 3 = 5 × 3 + 1 × 3",
        "words": [
          "decompose"
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "The Distributive Property",
        "teacher": true,
        "text": [
          "Decompose one factor into two easy facts. Then add.",
          "Sometimes ( ) go around each fact. It means the same thing."
        ],
        "pairs": [
          "Breaking 6 × 3 into (5 × 3) + (1 × 3) uses the Distributive Property.",
          "6 × 3 = 5 × 3 + 1 × 3"
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "Check",
        "item": {
          "id": "l1",
          "type": "choice",
          "prompt": "Which one shows 6 × 3 decomposed?",
          "target": "6 × 3",
          "options": [
            {
              "text": "5 × 3 + 1",
              "why": "The second part must be a whole fact: 1 × 3, not 1."
            },
            {
              "text": "5 × 3 + 1 × 3",
              "correct": true
            },
            {
              "text": "6 + 3",
              "why": "6 × 3 means 6 groups of 3, not 6 + 3."
            }
          ],
          "yes": "Yes! 5 × 3 + 1 × 3 is 15 + 3. That is 18."
        }
      }
    ]
  },
