      {
        "heading": "What your child is learning",
        "text": "To decompose (break apart) one factor of a multiplication fact into two easier facts, find each product, and add. Either factor can be decomposed: 6 × 3 = 5 × 3 + 1 × 3, or 6 × 3 = 6 × 2 + 6 × 1."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Try it with real things",
        "list": [
          "Set out 6 cups with 3 beans in each. Slide 1 cup away: 15 + 3 = 18.",
          "Now take 1 bean out of every cup into a second pile: 6 × 2 + 6 × 1 = 12 + 6 = 18."
        ]
      },
      {
        "heading": "Questions to ask",
        "list": [
          "What did you decompose?",
          "Why is 5 a good place to cut?",
          "Does a different cut change the answer?"
        ]
      },
      {
        "heading": "Common mistakes",
        "list": [
          "Adding the leftover number instead of its fact: 15 + 1 = 16 instead of 15 + 3.",
          "Stopping at the first part (15).",
          "Adding the factors: 6 + 3 = 9.",
          "Parts that don't add back to the factor, like 4 × 3 + 1 × 3 for 6 × 3.",
          "Changing the kept factor, like 5 × 3 + 1 × 1."
        ]
      },
      {
        "heading": "A note on notation",
        "text": "Your child's book writes the facts without ( ). The teacher's sentence uses ( ). Both mean the same thing. Study Scoop uses original practice questions; it never copies the textbook."
      }
    ]
